            "promptText": "Component 00a-06: StatusChip. Follow the board rules from the setup message.\n\nARTBOARDS (5, exact names)\n1. 00a · StatusChip · 01-anatomy · light: the pill with callouts, the glyph for each variant, the contrast table with the warning fail, ACCESSIBILITY, COPY, PLATFORMS, INSTEAD OF\n2. 00a · StatusChip · 02-variants-states · light: 9 chips × default, each on base, raised and app\n3. 00a · StatusChip · 04-in-context · light: f3-bills-list, the f3-members-roster invite row, the f8-seasonal-aha deadline\n4. 00a · StatusChip · 03-scaling-greyscale · light: the AX5 stacked bill row, 200%, web zoom, the greyscale grid and WRONG example, Increase Contrast\n5. 00a · StatusChip · 02-variants-states · dark: the same 27 cells in dark tokens\nAlso: update 00a · Notes.\n\nBATCH PLAN\nBatch 1 (this turn):\n 1. 00a · StatusChip · 01-anatomy · light\n 2. 00a · StatusChip · 02-variants-states · light\n 3. 00a · StatusChip · 04-in-context · light\n 4. 00a · StatusChip · 03-scaling-greyscale · light\n 5. 00a · StatusChip · 02-variants-states · dark\n 6. 00a · Notes (update it with the 06 entries below)\nThen stop and wait for the next component message.\n\nPURPOSE (print): One status per object (bills and invites), stated in words.\nUSED IN (print): f3-bills-list, f3-bill-detail-web, f3-members-roster, f8-seasonal-aha, f10-bill-provenance, f9-block-founders-panel.\n\nANATOMY (on 01-anatomy: callouts on \"Overdue\")\n- ① Pill, radius pill, height 24pt / 24dp / 24px.\n- ② Padding 8 horizontal, 4 gap between glyph and text.\n- ③ A leading 12pt glyph. It is the only place a semantic hue may appear. No glyph is a plain circle or ring (B8).\n- ④ Text in captionMedium 12/16/500, text.strong.\n- ⑤ Fill surface.sunken (dark: the sunken-fill rule in B6). No border by default.\n- ⑥ The chip is not a target; its row is. Print \"not a target\".\n\nGLYPH FOR EACH VARIANT (draw these exactly, in a labelled row on 01-anatomy)\n- Upcoming: a calendar outline, text.strong.\n- Due today: a clock face drawn as a rounded square with hands, not a circle. Light: text.strong (warning fails on sunken). Dark: semantic.warning.\n- Overdue: an exclamation mark inside a triangle outline, semantic.error (the error glyph).\n- Paid: a plain check stroke with no enclosing shape, semantic.success.\n- Expires: an hourglass, text.strong.\n- Declined: a × stroke, text.strong.\n- Waiting for your OK: a speech-bubble outline with three short dashes inside (not dots; B8), in primary.700 standing in for semantic.info (dark: primary.400 (dark link)).\n- Deadline chip: a calendar outline, text.strong. Deadline \"Today\": a calendar outline, text.strong in light and semantic.warning in dark.\n\nTOKEN AND CONTRAST TABLE (01-anatomy)\ntext.strong #374151 on sunken 9.37 · semantic.error #DC2626 on sunken 4.39 · semantic.success #059669 on sunken 3.42 · semantic.warning #D97706 on sunken 2.89 (FAILS, so light mode uses text.strong for warning glyphs) · primary.700 #0369a1 on sunken 5.39 · semantic.warning on dark raised 4.59 · semantic.error on dark raised 3.03 · semantic.success on dark raised 3.88 · primary.400 (dark link) #38BDF8 on dark raised 6.83 · dark text.strong #F1F5F9 on dark raised 13.35.\n\nVARIANT × STATE GRID (02-variants-states: 9 rows × 1 state; draw each chip on three backgrounds side by side: a surface.base row, a surface.raised card and a surface.app list)\nRows (specimen text → key). The specimen text is what the chip shows; the key never contains it:\n 1 \"Upcoming\" → upcoming\n 2 \"Due today\" → due-today\n 3 \"Overdue\" → overdue\n 4 \"Paid\" → paid\n 5 \"Expires in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\" → expires\n 6 \"Declined · Thu 15 Oct\" → declined\n 7 \"Waiting for your OK\" → waiting\n 8 \"in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\" (deadline chip; relative count first, as the house style and DateRow order it) → deadline\n 9 \"Today\" (deadline chip) → deadline-today\nLabel the column \"default (the only state)\", key: default. The three backgrounds show one published chip in three contexts; they are not separate variants, so print \"on surface.base\", \"on surface.raised\" or \"on surface.app\" in each cell label.\nDark twin: all 27 cells in dark tokens (dark base and dark app chips use dark surface.raised; the dark raised card uses dark surface.sunken; B6).\n\nSCALING AND GREYSCALE (03-scaling-greyscale)\n- AX5 (iOS 393): the bill row \"Comcast · $79.99 · due in 14 days · Mon 2 Nov\" with \"Upcoming\". The pair stacks, the chip moves under the title, and the chip becomes a wrapping rounded rectangle (radius lg 12) at 40pt with a 24pt glyph. Also draw chip 5 at AX5 to show the longest string wrapping.\n- 200% (Android 412): the same at 24sp.\n- Web 200% zoom (390): the same.\n- GREYSCALE (B10): every cell reads \"yes — the word carries the state\". Add a WRONG example: two chips that differ only in fill hue, with the fix \"fix: words, one chip\".\n- INCREASE CONTRAST: print \"Increase Contrast: nothing changes — chip text is already text.strong; glyph hues are decorative because the word carries the state\".\n\nACCESSIBILITY (print on 01-anatomy)\n- The text carries the state. It is read at the end of the row label: \"Comcast, 79 dollars 99, due in 14 days, Monday 2 November, Upcoming\". The glyph is decorative (hidden from assistive tech).\n- Non-colour: the word.\n- Target rule: the chip is never a target; the row is (B13).\n\nCOPY (print on 01-anatomy)\n- Exactly one chip per row.\n- Sentence case.\n- Dates show the weekday.\n- Beyond tomorrow, deadlines, due dates and expiries put the relative count first: \"in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\"; \"Expires in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\"; \"due in 4 days · Fri 23 Oct\". Tonight, today and tomorrow stand alone (\"Today\").\n- Past dates show the date only: \"Declined · Thu 15 Oct\".\n- Never use \"Paid\" as a verb in notifications; write \"marked paid\".\n\nPLATFORMS (print on 01-anatomy): no platform differences.\n\nIN CONTEXT (04-in-context: three crops)\n a) f3-bills-list, Home A, iOS 393: the rows \"Clark Public Utilities · $142.18 · due in 4 days · Fri 23 Oct\", \"City of Vancouver water · $84.00 · due in 9 days · Wed 28 Oct\" and \"Comcast · $79.99 · due in 14 days · Mon 2 Nov\", each with one \"Upcoming\" chip.\n b) f3-members-roster, Home A, web 390: \"Maya Chen · Owner\" and \"Sam Ortega · Member\" with NO chips, and an invite row \"priya@example.com\" with \"Expires in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\".\n c) f8-seasonal-aha, Place B, Android 412: \"Online or mail voter registration must arrive by Mon 26 Oct.\" with the deadline chip \"in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\", captioned \"Washington Secretary of State · statewide · as of Mon 19 Oct\" (a SourceCaption with the official mark).\n\nINSTEAD OF (print on 01-anatomy)\n- Instead of warning-hue text on a warning tint, use text.strong on surface.sunken with the hue only on the glyph — because warning text on its tint is about 3:1.\n- Instead of two chips on one row, pick the one status that matters — because two chips compete and get misread.\n- Instead of a status chip on a person (\"Verified\", \"Active\"), state the role in plain text — because people are not statuses.\n- Instead of a warning-tinted countdown pill (founding window), draw a neutral date chip — because countdowns pressure people.\n- Instead of the date before the count (\"Mon 26 Oct · in 7 days\"), write \"in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\" — because the house style and DateRow lead with the count, and every surface copies this chip.\n- Instead of a disc-shaped status dot, use the listed glyphs — because circles belong to ProvenanceMark.\n\nPUBLISH KEYS\nVariants: upcoming · due-today · overdue · paid · expires · declined · waiting · deadline · deadline-today. State: default. <size|platform> is \"any\". Publish one name per chip per theme; the three backgrounds are one chip. Examples: \"StatusChip / overdue / default / any / light\" · \"StatusChip / deadline / default / any / dark\".\n\nNOTES ENTRIES (subheading \"06 StatusChip\")\n 06.A1 Warning glyphs (Due today, deadline Today) are text.strong in light mode (semantic.warning is 2.89:1 on sunken) and semantic.warning in dark mode (4.59:1). The \"Waiting for your OK\" glyph uses primary.700 in place of semantic.info.\n 06.A2 Deadline-chip ORDER CHANGED: the source spec writes the date first (\"Oct 26 · 18 days\"). This board puts the relative count first (\"in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\"), matching the house style, the research brief (section 6) and DateRow (\"in 11 days · Fri 30 Oct\"). If the founder wants date-first for chips only, say so before publishing, because all 59 surfaces copy this chip.\n 06.A3 The invite expiry chip also leads with the count: \"Expires in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\". Alternative if rejected: \"Expires Mon 26 Oct\".\n 06.A4 Source example strings were re-dated to the fixtures: \"Expires Sep 23\" → \"Expires in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\"; \"Declined · Sep 15\" → \"Declined · Thu 15 Oct\"; \"Oct 26 · 18 days\" → \"in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\".\n 06.B1 \"Declined · Thu 15 Oct\" · \"in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\" (house-style pattern) · \"Expires in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\" · \"Washington Secretary of State · statewide · as of Mon 19 Oct\" · the bill rows (\"Clark Public Utilities · $142.18 · due in 4 days · Fri 23 Oct\"; \"City of Vancouver water · $84.00 · due in 9 days · Wed 28 Oct\"; \"Comcast · $79.99 · due in 14 days · Mon 2 Nov\") · \"fix: words, one chip\".\n 06.B2 Spoken label: \"Comcast, 79 dollars 99, due in 14 days, Monday 2 November, Upcoming\".\n 06.C List any cell you could not draw, or \"none\".\n 06.D Deadline chip: count first (as drawn) or date first? (06.A2)\n 06.E Every StatusChip name (9 chips × light and dark = 18 names).\n\nDONE WHEN\n- All 5 artboards exist with their exact names, and Notes has the 06 entries with the earlier entries unchanged.\n- Both grids have all 9 rows, each on three backgrounds, with printed keys; no key contains a fixture string or date.\n- The deadline chip reads \"in 7 days · Mon 26 Oct\" everywhere it appears, and every date row leads with the relative count beyond tomorrow.\n- Chip text is text.strong on sunken in every cell. Semantic hues appear only on glyphs, and no glyph is a plain disc or ring.\n- Each row in the crops has at most one chip, and the people rows have none."
