            ],
            "promptText": "Component 00a-09: ChoiceChip. Follow the board rules (B1–B18) from the setup message. This is the last component; after this batch the board is complete.\n\nARTBOARDS AND BATCH PLAN (this turn, in this order, exact names)\n 1. 00a · ChoiceChip · 01-anatomy · light: callouts, the token table, the per-platform R3 and R5 forms, ACCESSIBILITY, COPY, PLATFORMS, INSTEAD OF\n 2. 00a · ChoiceChip · 02-variants-states · light: the 5 × 5 grid\n 3. 00a · ChoiceChip · 04-in-context · light: three crops\n 4. 00a · ChoiceChip · 03-scaling-greyscale · light: AX5, Android 200%, web zoom (390 and 1440), GREYSCALE with a WRONG example, INCREASE CONTRAST\n 5. 00a · ChoiceChip · 02-variants-states · dark: the same 25 cells in dark tokens\n 6. 00a · Notes: add the 09 entries below (final version)\nThen stop and say the board is complete.\n\nPURPOSE (print): Small single-choice chips for channel, role, time and filter; they are never status badges.\nUSED IN (print): f3-invite-composer, f4-briefing-optin-card, f10-mail-piece-photo, f3-bills-list, f4-notification-settings.\n\nANATOMY (01-anatomy; callouts on the time group \"6:30 AM · 7:00 AM · 7:30 AM · 8:00 AM\", 7:00 AM selected)\n① Height 32pt / 32dp / 32px visual inside a target (B13). Draw both outlines.\n② Radius pill; padding 12 horizontal; 8 gap between chips.\n③ Label 13/18/600.\n④ Selected: fill primary.50; 1px primary.700 outline (5.93:1 on base, so the shape stays visible although the fill is only 1.07:1); text primary.700 (5.57:1 on the fill); a leading 12pt check stroke in primary.700 with a 4pt gap. Dark: fill primary.900, 1px primary.400 (dark link) outline, text dark text.primary, check primary.400 (dark link).\n⑤ Unselected: 1px outline in text.secondary (dark: dark text.secondary) and text.strong label. Print \"spec border.strong → text.secondary (see Notes)\": border.strong is 1.47:1, and the house style says an input's only edge uses text.secondary ink or darker.\n⑥ Focus: the B14 ring, shown on an unselected chip and on a selected chip.\n⑦ Disabled: text and 1px outline both text.muted (dark: dark text.muted), no check, and a reason caption under the group in text.secondary. No border token is the edge. Print \"disabled controls are exempt from WCAG contrast; the reason caption carries the meaning (see Notes)\".\n\nTOKEN AND CONTRAST TABLE (01-anatomy)\nprimary.700 #0369a1 on primary.50 5.57, on base 5.93 (selected outline) · primary.50 #f0f9ff on base 1.07 (fill only) · text.strong #374151 on base 10.31 · text.secondary #6B7280 4.83 (unselected outline) · text.muted #9CA3AF 2.54 (disabled text and outline) · border.strong #D1D5DB 1.47 (not used as the edge) · border.focus #0284c7 4.10 · dark: text.primary #E5E7EB on primary.900 #0c4a6e 7.64 · primary.400 (dark link) #38BDF8 outline 8.33 on dark base and 6.83 on dark raised, check 4.42 on primary.900 · dark text.secondary #94A3B8 outline 6.96 · dark text.muted #64748B 3.75 on dark base (disabled).\n\nVARIANT × STATE GRID (02-variants-states: 5 rows × 5 columns, drawn in the web and Android chip form)\nColumns (label → key): default → default · selected → selected · disabled → disabled · focus → focus · selected + focus → selected-focus (shows that selection and focus look different).\nIn R1–R4 the disabled cell disables every chip in the group and prints the row's reason caption under it. R5 differs.\n R1 channel → channel: \"Email\" · \"Username\" · \"Share instead\". default: none selected. selected: \"Email\". disabled: \"You're offline. Invites need a connection.\" focus: on \"Username\". selected-focus: \"Email\".\n R2 role → role: \"Member\" · \"Guest\". A caption 12/16 text.secondary capability line under the group describes the selected role only. Member: \"Sees and edits the calendar, sees bills and who paid, sees who lives here\". Guest: \"Same as Member until Sun 1 Nov · in 13 days\" (no cell selects Guest).\n   default: none selected, no capability line; cell note \"caption appears after a role is chosen\". selected: \"Member\" + Member line. disabled: both disabled, no capability line, reason \"You're offline. Invites need a connection.\" focus: on \"Guest\", none selected, no capability line. selected-focus: \"Member\" + Member line.\n R3 time → time: \"6:30 AM\" · \"7:00 AM\" · \"7:30 AM\" · \"8:00 AM\". default: none selected. selected: \"7:00 AM\". disabled: \"You're offline. Your briefing time will save when you're back online.\" focus: on \"7:30 AM\". selected-focus: \"7:00 AM\".\n R4 filter → filter: \"Photographed\", one toggle chip. default: off. selected: on. disabled: \"You're offline. Filters need a connection.\" focus: off. selected-focus: on.\n R5 segment → segment: \"Upcoming\" · \"Paid\" · \"All\". default: \"Upcoming\" selected; cell note \"a segment always has one selected\". selected: \"Upcoming\". disabled: \"Upcoming\" stays enabled and selected (cached bills still show); \"Paid\" and \"All\" disabled; reason \"You're offline. Paid and All need a connection.\" focus: \"Upcoming\" selected, focus on \"Paid\". selected-focus: \"Upcoming\".\nDark twin: all 25 cells with the dark tokens from ④, ⑤ and ⑦.\n\nPER-PLATFORM FORMS (01-anatomy, side by side, light only)\nR3 and R5, each in the default and selected states, on ios, android and web = 12 drawings. iOS R5 is the native segmented control (UISegmentedControl look, 32pt, selected segment raised); R3 on iOS is a Pantopus chip. Android uses Material 3 filter chips (leading check when selected, 32dp visual, 48dp target) with Pantopus tokens and the pill radius instead of the M3 8dp corner; Android R5 is a single-select row of those chips. Web: chips as specified.\n\nSCALING AND GREYSCALE (03-scaling-greyscale)\nLarge-text frames draw R1, R2 (Member selected, capability line under the selected row), R3 and R5 as one-column rows. R4 stays a single chip that grows with its 44pt / 26sp label and becomes a rounded rectangle (radius lg 12) when it wraps (B9).\n- AX5 (iOS 393): each option is a full-width row, at least 44pt tall, label 44pt, wrapping. Selected row: primary.50 fill, label primary.700, trailing 24pt check stroke in primary.700. Unselected rows: no fill, no indicator. No ring. R5's segmented control becomes these rows too.\n- 200% (Android 412): rows at least 48dp, label 26sp, Material 3 radio on the leading edge (the platform control; flagged B8 exception). Selected row: primary.50 fill, label primary.700, a 24dp radio with a 2dp primary.700 ring and a 12dp filled primary.700 centre, no trailing check. Unselected rows: no fill, label text.strong, a 24dp empty radio with a 2dp text.secondary ring.\n- Web 200% zoom, two frames. 390 wide (195 px effective, B9): below the 360 px switch point, so draw the AX5 row form (trailing check, no ring), built as role=\"radio\" rows. 1440 wide (720 px effective), cropped to the group: the chips wrap onto several lines and stay chips. Print \"switch to rows below 360 px effective width\".\n- GREYSCALE (B10): each cell reads \"yes — selected = check + fill + outline; focus = ring\"; disabled cells read \"yes — reason caption\". Add a WRONG example: a selected chip that differs only by hue, with \"fix: add the check\".\n- INCREASE CONTRAST (B16): R3 with unselected outlines in text.strong, and the Android unselected radio rings in text.strong. Selected chips are unchanged; disabled chips stay text.muted (B16).\n\nIN CONTEXT (04-in-context)\n a) f3-invite-composer, iOS 393, Home A: channel chips (Email selected), the field \"priya@example.com\", role chips (Member selected) with the Member capability line, then the primary button \"Send invite\".\n b) f4-briefing-optin-card, Android 412: \"A morning heads-up?\", the time chips (7:00 AM selected), then three equal-weight buttons \"Yes\", \"Not now\" and \"No thanks\". Label \"Yes\" with the note \"opens the OS dialog; no second Yes\".\n c) f3-bills-list, Home A: \"Upcoming / Paid / All\" as chips in a web 1440 crop (720 wide), and as the segmented control on iOS 393.\n\nACCESSIBILITY (print on 01-anatomy)\n- Selection is shown by check, fill and outline, not colour alone. The focus ring is separate from selection.\n- R1, R2, R3 and R5 are radio groups. iOS: buttons with the selected trait (\"7:00 AM, selected, 2 of 4\"). Android: selectable semantics (\"7:00 AM, selected\"). Web: role=\"radiogroup\" with role=\"radio\" and aria-checked; arrow keys move between chips.\n- R4 is not a radio group; it is a toggle. Web: a button with aria-pressed. iOS: a button with the selected trait. Android: toggleable semantics. Spoken: \"Photographed, off\" / \"Photographed, on\".\n- Disabled chips stay focusable and read their reason.\n- Targets: a target (B13) around the 32 visual, with 8pt gaps.\n\nCOPY (print on 01-anatomy): Sentence case; exact strings as in the grid. Times are written \"7:00 AM\" (space, capital AM), never \"6:00pm\" or \"7:40 am\".\n\nPLATFORMS (print on 01-anatomy): iOS: R5 is the native segmented control; R1–R4 are Pantopus chips. Android: Material 3 filter chips with the pill radius; R5 is a single-select chip row. Web: :focus-visible ring, no hover-only affordance. Large text: trailing-check rows on iOS and web; Material 3 radios on Android.\n\nINSTEAD OF (print on 01-anatomy)\n- Instead of a ChoiceChip as a status badge, use StatusChip — because a choice chip looks tappable.\n- Instead of a status chip on a person, write the role in plain text — because people are not statuses.\n- Instead of a border token as any chip's only edge, use text.secondary (unselected) or text.muted (disabled) — because an input's edge must be visible.\n- Instead of a selected chip bounded only by its 1.07:1 fill, add the 1px primary.700 outline — as AddressChip's selected edge does.\n- Instead of the focus ring doubling as selection, keep the ring for focus and fill + check + outline for selection — because they are different states.\n- Instead of truncating long labels at large text, switch to one-column rows — because truncated choices cannot be told apart.\n- Instead of an empty-ring radio on iOS or web rows, use a trailing check on the selected row — because iOS has no radio control and an empty ring is the unconfirmed mark's shape.\n- Instead of disabling a segment that still has cached content, disable only the segments that need a connection — because the reason must match what is disabled.\n\nPUBLISH KEYS\nVariants: channel · role · time · filter · segment. States: default · selected · disabled · focus · selected-focus. <size|platform> is \"any\" for all 50 grid cells (25 light + 25 dark). The 12 per-platform drawings are \"ChoiceChip / {time,segment} / {default,selected} / {ios,android,web} / light\". ChoiceChip total: 62 names. Examples: \"ChoiceChip / time / selected / any / dark\" · \"ChoiceChip / segment / selected / ios / light\".\n\nNOTES ENTRIES (subheading \"09 ChoiceChip\")\n 09.A1 Unselected outline text.secondary instead of spec border.strong (see ⑤).\n 09.A2 Disabled text and outline text.muted, no border token; exempt from WCAG contrast; the reason caption carries the meaning (see ⑦).\n 09.A3 Selected chip adds a 1px primary.700 outline (dark primary.400); the spec names only the fill (see ④).\n 09.A4 R5 disabled keeps \"Upcoming\" enabled and selected; only \"Paid\" and \"All\" are disabled (see R5).\n 09.A5 R2 shows the capability line only for the selected role (see R2).\n 09.A6 R5 default shows \"Upcoming\" selected, because a segment always has one selection.\n 09.A7 Android chips keep the pill radius instead of the M3 8dp corner.\n 09.A8 Android 200% rows use Material 3 radios, including the empty ring (same flagged exception as 07.A1 (b)); iOS and web rows use a trailing check and no ring.\n 09.A9 Web zoom has two frames: 390 (rows) and 1440 at 200% (wrapping chips).\n 09.A10 The grid adds a selected-focus column, not in the contract's four states, to show that selection and focus look different.\n 09.B1 Invented: \"You're offline. Invites need a connection.\" · \"You're offline. Your briefing time will save when you're back online.\" · \"You're offline. Filters need a connection.\" · \"You're offline. Paid and All need a connection.\" · \"Send invite\" · \"caption appears after a role is chosen\" · \"a segment always has one selected\" · \"switch to rows below 360 px effective width\" · \"fix: add the check\" · \"opens the OS dialog; no second Yes\".\n 09.B2 Spoken labels: \"7:00 AM, selected, 2 of 4\" · \"7:00 AM, selected\" · \"Photographed, off\" · \"Photographed, on\".\n 09.B3 Sourced, not invented: the Member capability line (f3-invite-composer research); \"Same as Member until Sun 1 Nov · in 13 days\" (adapted from f3-invite-composer research, date re-fixtured); \"6:30 AM · 7:00 AM · 7:30 AM · 8:00 AM\" and \"Yes\" / \"Not now\" (f4-briefing-optin-card); \"No thanks\" (brief section 7, the lasting decline); \"A morning heads-up?\" (glossary).\n 09.C List any cell you could not draw, or \"none\".\n 09.D Add to 07.D: the same Android radio question applies to ChoiceChip's 200% rows.\n 09.E All 62 ChoiceChip names, then a closing count of every name in section E.\n\nDONE WHEN\n- All 5 artboards exist with their exact names. Notes has the 09 entries, every earlier entry is unchanged, and sections A–E are complete for all nine components.\n- Both grids are 5 × 5 with printed keys, and every cell is drawn.\n- Every selected chip has the fill, the leading check and the 1px outline. Focus is shown by the ring only.\n- No chip uses a border token as its only edge.\n- The R5 disabled cell disables only \"Paid\" and \"All\".\n- Crop b shows \"Yes\", \"Not now\" and \"No thanks\" at equal weight.\n- Only the Android 200% frame draws radio rings. The web 390 frame shows rows, and the 1440 frame shows wrapping chips.\n- Section E lists 62 ChoiceChip names and gives the closing count."
          }
        ],
        "clean": true
      },
      {
        "prompts": [
          {
            "id": "00b-00",
            "promptText": "Use the Pantopus house style pasted above. This project is Foundations board 00b: Data instruments. This message sets up the board. Don't draw anything yet.\n\nWHAT THIS BOARD IS FOR\nThis project draws seven components as reference specimens, not as screens: ScaleStrip, AqiBand, FourteenDayStrip, YearBand, SlotMeter, PublicPointMap and FactCount. Later screen prompts, sent from separate projects that can't see this one, ask for these components by exact name. Once published, these specimens are the only definition of each component. Draw every number, token and string exactly as the component messages give it.\n\nHOW THIS PROJECT RUNS\n- This message holds the board rules: G1–G8 and R1–R12. They apply to every artboard in this project. Later messages call them \"the board rules from the setup message\".\n- After this message, I'll send one component message per turn, in this order: ScaleStrip, AqiBand, FourteenDayStrip, YearBand, SlotMeter, PublicPointMap, FactCount.\n- ScaleStrip and AqiBand each arrive in two parts (part 1, then part 2). The two parts count as one component message. Part 2 relies on everything in part 1.\n- Each component message (or part) includes its ARTBOARDS list and a BATCH PLAN. Draw only the first batch. Then stop and wait until I type \"continue\" or send the next part. Never start another component's artboards early.\n- The last batch of every component updates 00b · Notes (R12).\n- Everything you need is in the house style, this message, and the current and earlier component messages in this project. Don't open other projects.\n\nPUBLISH (my step, after I accept a component)\nWhen I accept a component, I add it to the Pantopus design system under its exact name. That includes its light and dark specimens and every variant and state. Your job is to make that step mechanical:\n- Give every drawn grid cell and every named extra specimen a publish name (R10).\n- List the names on 00b · Notes.\n- Stand-ins (R11) and anything marked \"do not publish\" never get a publish name.\n\nSHARED GRAMMAR (all seven instruments)\nG1. Each instrument states its fact in words and numbers. The graphic repeats the fact and never replaces it.\nG2. Nothing adds up to a grade, score, percentage or verdict. No progress ring, completion ring, countdown, streak or confetti appears anywhere. An authority's own band names are allowed even when they contain a percentage; each component message lists the allowed ones. \"N of M\" appears only where a component message allows it.\nG3. Nothing depends on colour alone. The EPA AQI hues are the only sanctioned colour scale, and even there the category is printed in words.\nG4. Three shapes mean provenance (00a · ProvenanceMark) and nothing else: a solid disc (Official), a ring (On record, not confirmed) and a solid disc with a knocked-out tick (You added this). No position marker, pointer, bullet, callout end, segment or badge on this board is a plain disc or ring. A component message that needs an exception describes it.\nG5. A mark, cell or segment is never a tap target on its own. The target is the row, the whole instrument or a labelled button.\nG6. Each instrument has a one-sentence summary and a spoken label, and both are printed on its artboards. The same facts also appear nearby as rows or a list.\nG7. Every provenance mark keeps at least 3:1 contrast in every state. Staleness is always stated in words.\nG8. Widgets and the share card are standalone surfaces. They print the provenance word next to each mark or in a legend line inside the block.\n\nBOARD RULES\nR1 · Artboard format and naming.\n- Name every artboard \"00b · <Component> · <NN-name> · <light|dark>\", exactly as the component's ARTBOARDS list gives it. This overrides the house-style artboard pattern, because one specimen artboard shows several platforms. The last artboard is \"00b · Notes\".\n- Each artboard is 1440 wide and as tall as its content. Light artboards use surface.app; dark artboards use dark surface.app.\n- Above each artboard, print its exact name in label 13/18/600 text.strong.\n- At the top inside each artboard, print three lines, as the component message gives them:\n  - the component name, in h1 30/36/700 text.primary\n  - its purpose line, in body 16/24 text.secondary\n  - \"Used in: <surface ids>\", in caption 12/16 text.secondary\n- Section headings use overline 11/16/600 caps, for example ANATOMY, VARIANT × STATE, AX5 AND 200%, GREYSCALE, IN CONTEXT, ACCESSIBILITY and INSTEAD OF.\n\nR2 · Frames and cards.\n- Draw specimens at true size (1pt = 1px on the artboard) inside plain frames, named as the message names them: iOS 393, Android 412, web-390, web-1440, and the widget outlines 338×158 and 338×354.\n- In the three phone frames, draw the specimen card 358 wide with 16pt padding, which leaves 326pt of content width. Left-align the card at a 16pt frame margin. The right margin is then 19pt in iOS 393, 16pt in web-390 and 38pt in Android 412.\n- All phone geometry assumes 326pt of content width unless a message says otherwise.\n- Light cards use surface.base, radius lg 12, shadow sm and 16pt padding.\n- Dark cards use dark surface.raised with a 1px dark border.strong decorative edge. The card edge carries no meaning, so the 3:1 minimum doesn't apply to it.\n- Each component message gives its own web-1440 card width.\n\nR3 · Units.\n- A size given in pt is the same number in dp (Android) and px (web).\n- Only target minimums differ by platform: 44pt on iOS, 48dp with 8dp gaps on Android, and 44px on web.\n- Spacing uses the 4px scale: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 40, 48, 64.\n- A few micro offsets inside instruments are deliberately off that scale: 6, 3, 2 and 1pt. Use them only where a component message states them, and never for layout spacing.\n- Type uses only these sizes:\n  - the house type scale\n  - medium (500) weights of body, bodySmall and caption at the same sizes: bodyMedium, bodySmallMedium and captionMedium\n  - the R7 sizes\n  - the 11pt widget minimum\n  - OG sizes, where a component message states them\n\nR4 · Callouts.\n- Callouts sit in an annotation gutter outside the UI, in caption 12/16 text.strong. Gutter labels (\"specimen\", \"specimen value\", \"stand-in — …\") use the same style.\n- Leader lines are 1px text.secondary and end in a short perpendicular tick, never a dot (G4).\n- Dimension lines are 1px text.secondary with 4pt end ticks.\n- Each callout names the part, its size and its token, for example \"Track · 12pt · fill surface.raised · keyline 1px text.secondary\".\n- Callouts appear on 01-anatomy artboards and wherever a message asks for them.\n\nR5 · Tokens and dark mode.\n- Label every colour by its token name. Hex values appear on the board only in contrast tables a message asks for and in the AQI swatch row.\n- The dark hex values in these rules and in the component messages are for drawing. In callouts, print the token name followed by \"(dark)\", for example \"surface.raised (dark)\".\n- On dark artboards, use exactly this mapping:\n  - surfaces: surface.base → #0F172A · surface.raised → #1E293B · surface.app → #020617 · surface.sunken → #0F172A · surface.muted → #111827\n  - text: text.primary → #E5E7EB · text.secondary → #94A3B8 · text.strong → #F1F5F9\n  - borders: border.default → #1F2937 and border.strong → #374151 (both are decoration only) · border.focus → #38BDF8\n  - primary.700 → #38BDF8. This applies to links, text buttons and any edge a message names, because primary.700 is only 2.47:1 on dark raised.\n- These tokens keep their light values in dark mode and are labelled by token name:\n  - the primary.600 fill (3.57:1 on dark raised)\n  - semantic.error glyphs (3.03:1)\n  - semantic.warning glyphs (4.59:1)\n- Semantic hues go on glyphs only.\n- On dark cards, surface.sunken fills (#0F172A on #1E293B) look darker than the card. This is intended, because keylines and text carry the edges.\n- A sunken-filled shape that sits directly on dark base uses dark surface.raised instead, so that it stays visible.\n- Never use the light infoBg wash in dark mode, because dark text.primary on it is only 1.16:1. Component messages give the dark highlight.\n- The EPA AQI hues never change in dark mode.\n- This board draws no selection state.\n\nR6 · Contrast numbers (WCAG 2). Opacities are alpha-blended over their surface first. Use these numbers and don't recompute them. Print only the rows a message asks for. A \"tinted cell\" is surface.sunken at 40% over base.\nLight:\n- text.secondary: 4.83 on base · 4.63 on raised · 4.51 on app · 4.39 on sunken. That is below 4.5, so text on sunken uses text.strong.\n- text.strong: 9.37 on sunken · 10.31 on base\n- text.primary: 17.74 on base · 16.12 on sunken\n- primary.700: 5.93 on base · 5.68 on raised · 5.39 on sunken\n- primary.600 fill: 4.10 on base\n- glyphs: semantic.error 4.83 on base · semantic.warning 3.19 on base\n- stale mark (the 00a stale variant, text.primary at 50%): 3.39 on base · 3.36 on raised · 3.34 on app · 3.32 on sunken\n- on a tinted cell: text.secondary 4.65 · stale mark 3.36\n- Never use text.secondary at 50% or text.muted for readable text. Both fail 4.5:1.\nDark:\n- text.secondary: 6.96 on dark base · 5.71 on dark raised\n- text.primary: 11.82 on dark raised · 14.42 on dark base\n- text.strong: 16.30 on dark base · 13.35 on dark raised\n- #38BDF8: 6.83 on dark raised · 8.33 on dark base\n- stale mark (dark text.primary at 50%): 4.45 on dark base · 4.10 on dark raised · 4.48 on dark app\n- on a dark tinted cell: text.secondary 6.23 · stale mark 4.27\n- decoration only: dark border.strong 1.42 on dark raised · dark border.default 1.00 on dark raised\n\nR7 · AX5 and 200%.\n- iOS AX5 sizes: h1 58 · h2 56 · h3 55 · body and bodyMedium 53 · bodySmall and bodySmallMedium 49 · label 44 · caption and captionMedium 43 · overline 40.\n- Android 200% sizes (Android 14 non-linear scaling): h1 38 · h2 36 · h3 34 · body 28 · bodySmall 26 · label 25 · caption 24 · overline 22. Medium weights use the same sizes as their base styles.\n- Web: 200% browser zoom on web-1440, which gives an effective layout 720 CSS px wide.\n- Graphic geometry keeps its size, and text reflows around it. This covers tracks, cells, segments, markers, glyph-only buttons, and marks inside cells, lanes and tracks.\n- Marks inside text lines (captions, rows, legends) scale with their text.\n- Text wraps and never truncates or ends in an ellipsis. Side-by-side pairs stack. Widgets fall back to text only, as each message states.\n- Draw iOS AX5 in a 393 frame and Android 200% in a 412 frame, each as tall as needed.\n\nR8 · Greyscale.\n- Redraw the listed specimens at 100% desaturation.\n- Under each one, print in caption text.secondary the non-colour cue that still carries the meaning, in the words the component message gives.\n\nR9 · Grids and keys.\n- A variant × state grid has variant rows and state columns, in the order the message gives.\n- Print each row and column label in label 13/18/600 text.strong. Under each label, print its key as \"key: <key>\" in caption 12/16 text.secondary.\n- A key is lowercase and hyphenated. It never contains spaces, \"/\", a fixture string or a date. Each component message lists its keys; use them exactly.\n- Each cell holds one specimen at true size, or the stacked specimens the message asks for. Under the specimen, print the cell's keys, for example \"detail · stale\".\n- A combination that has no meaning prints \"n/a — <reason>\" in caption text.secondary and nothing else. Use the one reason the message gives. Never leave a cell blank.\n- Every specimen outside a grid gets the extra key the message gives. So does a specimen stacked inside a cell that the message gives its own extra key.\n\nR10 · Publish names. Give one name to each drawn grid cell and each extra specimen, in one of two forms:\n- \"<Component> / <variant key> / <state key> / <light|dark>\"\n- \"<Component> / extra / <extra key> / <light|dark>\"\nNames use keys only. n/a cells get no name.\n\nR11 · Borrowed parts and stand-ins.\n- These instruments use parts from boards 00a, 00c and 00d. Use the published part when the design system has it.\n- Otherwise, draw the stand-in described below and label it in the gutter \"stand-in — the 00a/00c/00d specimen replaces it\".\n- Don't develop a stand-in beyond this description, and never give it a publish name.\nThe stand-ins:\n- 00a · ProvenanceMark: a mark at XS 12pt or S 16pt, in text.secondary at full opacity.\n  - Official: a solid disc.\n  - On record, not confirmed: a ring with a stroke of at least 1.5px.\n  - You added this: a solid disc with a knocked-out tick (a true cut-out).\n  - Stale variant: the same shape in text.primary at 50%, always paired with age words.\n- 00a · SourceCaption: one caption 12/16 line in text.secondary (text.strong on sunken), in the pattern [ProvenanceMark S] authority · scope word · as-of. It wraps and never truncates.\n- 00a · FreshnessLine: one caption line in text.secondary that states the age in words, with an optional 16pt leading glyph.\n- 00a · ScopeChip: a 24pt pill on surface.sunken, holding a 12pt person or house glyph and the label in label 13/18 text.strong.\n- 00a · KindGlyph: a 24pt line icon with a 2pt stroke, in text.strong.\n- 00c · InlineErrorRow: a row in the component's own slot, with no tinted background. It holds:\n  - a 16pt error glyph (semantic.error on the glyph only)\n  - the sentence, in body text.primary\n  - a 44pt \"Retry\" text button in primary.700\n- 00c · DateRow: a row at least 56pt tall. It holds:\n  - a leading KindGlyph\n  - the title, in body text.primary\n  - a trailing ProvenanceMark S\n  - line 2, in caption text.secondary, with the relative and absolute date (\"Tomorrow\", or \"in 4 days · Fri 23 Oct\") and a short SourceCaption\n- 00c · FactRow: a 56pt row. It holds the label in bodySmall text.secondary above the value in body text.primary, a ProvenanceMark and a trailing chevron.\n- 00c · TextActionRow (outbound): a row at least 44pt tall with a 1px text.secondary edge, a verb-led label in primary.700 and a trailing \"↗\".\n- 00d · WarmingSkeleton: surface.sunken shapes with the component's final geometry and radius, and no text.\n  - It reserves the exact final height.\n  - It shimmers by default and is static under Reduce Motion.\n- 00d · QuietDayReceipt: draw only the form a component message describes, exactly as described.\n\nR12 · Fixtures, motion, focus and 00b · Notes.\n- Use the house-style FIXTURES exactly. TODAY is Mon 19 Oct 2026, 6:10 PM Pacific.\n- Draw every quoted string exactly as written.\n- Label any value that isn't tied to a fixture address \"specimen value\" in the gutter.\n- Show motion as static annotated frames, never as a loop. Annotate Reduce Motion wherever it changes something.\n- Keyboard focus on web is a 2px border.focus outline with a 2px offset around the focusable row, strip or button (#38BDF8 in dark).\n- 00b · Notes is one light artboard, 1440 wide.\n  - It has sections A to G. Divide each section by component, with the component name as an h3 subheading.\n  - Create it in the last batch of the first component.\n  - Each later component appends its entries in its own last batch and keeps everything already there.\n- The sections:\n  - A. Invented strings: every quoted string that is not in the house-style fixtures or the component contract.\n  - B. Assumptions.\n  - C. Normalisations.\n  - D. Deviations from the contract. I sign off on each one.\n  - E. Open questions for the founder, numbered across the whole board.\n  - F. n/a cells, each with its reason.\n  - G. Publish names (R10).\n- Start section B with a \"Board\" subsection that records:\n  - the artboard naming override (R1)\n  - the off-scale micro offsets, and the medium type weights (R3)\n  - the 358-wide phone cards, left-aligned at a 16pt frame margin, with right margins of 19pt (iOS 393), 16pt (web-390) and 38pt (Android 412) (R2)\n  - the decorative dark card edge in border.strong, and the dark sunken fills (R2, R5)\n  - the dark values for surface.sunken, surface.muted, text.strong and border.strong, which the house style does not define and this board assumes (R5)\n  - stale marks, drawn as the 00a stale variant (text.primary at 50%), which passes 3:1 (R6, R11). This is the only place the board records the stale treatment; components don't repeat it.\n\nReply 'ready' and wait for the first component.",
            "component": null
          },
          {
